## Docker + PostgreSQL

The container stack runs PostgreSQL 16 and the site (gunicorn with 3 workers, WhiteNoise for static files, DEBUG and
the debug toolbar off, production image without dev packages) as compose project `{{PROJECT_PACKAGE}}`.

    bin/docker-up.sh               # creates .env if absent, builds, starts, waits until healthy, prints the URL
    bin/docker-down.sh             # stops; keeps data volumes
    bin/docker-down.sh --volumes   # stops AND deletes this project's database/media/static volumes

- **Ports:** the app is published on `127.0.0.1:${APP_PORT:-8889}` (`APP_PORT=8891 bin/docker-up.sh`, or set it
  in `.env`; override the address with `APP_BIND`). PostgreSQL publishes no host port.
- **CSRF origin and hosts:** admin login fails with a CSRF 403 unless the origin you browse to is trusted. The
  default follows `APP_PORT` for `localhost`/`127.0.0.1`; for any other host name or a TLS proxy set
  `DJANGO_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` (with scheme and port) and `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS` in `.env`. `localhost` and
  `127.0.0.1` are always allowed (the container healthcheck uses `127.0.0.1`).
- **Secrets:** `bin/docker-env.sh` writes `.env` (mode 600) with a random `DB_PASSWORD` and
  `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY` only if `.env` does not exist; it never overwrites or prints them, and writes nothing at
  all when it cannot generate them (needs `python3` or `openssl`). `.env` is gitignored. See `.env.example`.
- **First run:** `SEED_ON_START=1` in `.env` seeds a FRESH database once (`manage.py seed --first-run-only`:
  skipped when the home page exists, so a restart never refills a placeholder an editor emptied on purpose).
  Re-seed deliberately with `docker compose exec app python manage.py seed`. Then
  `docker compose exec app python manage.py createsuperuser`.
- **Shared cache:** with PostgreSQL the default cache is a database table (`django_cache`, created at start by
  `createcachetable`) shared by all gunicorn workers. A per-process cache served a stale menu after
  publishing (navigation present on some requests only). `DJANGO_CACHE=locmem|db` overrides it.
- **Content that predates versioning** (pages without a version row render as missing): set
  `CREATE_VERSIONS=1` and `CREATE_VERSIONS_USER=<username>` once, restart, then unset `CREATE_VERSIONS`.
- **Health:** `GET /health/` returns `{"status": "ok"}` after a cheap `SELECT 1` (503 if the database is
  down); the compose healthcheck uses it.
- **Media:** uploads live in the `media` volume, including filer's private files (`media/filer_private/`, not
  served under `/media/`). With DEBUG off Django does not serve uploads unless `DJANGO_SERVE_MEDIA=1` (the
  compose default). Behind a real proxy serve `/media/` there and set it to 0.
- **Behind a TLS proxy:** `DJANGO_BEHIND_PROXY=1` trusts `X-Forwarded-Proto` and marks the session, CSRF and
  language cookies Secure; optional `DJANGO_HSTS_SECONDS`, `DJANGO_HSTS_INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS` and
  `DJANGO_SSL_REDIRECT` (all off by default; `/health/` is exempt from the redirect). **Warning:** never set
  `APP_BIND=0.0.0.0` together with `DJANGO_BEHIND_PROXY=1` unless only the proxy can reach the port,
  otherwise any client can forge `X-Forwarded-Proto`.
- **Logs:** `docker compose logs -f app`.
- **Backup:** `bin/docker-backup.sh [DIR]` writes `db.sql` and `media.tar.gz` (default `backups/<UTC time>`, mode
  600, refuses an existing directory). The stack must be running; keep backups private.
- **Restore:** on a fresh stack start ONLY the database, load the dump with `ON_ERROR_STOP`, then start the app
  (restoring into a database the app already migrated fails with hundreds of "already exists" errors).
  `bin/docker-restore.sh DIR` does exactly that and refuses a non-empty database; `--wipe` additionally runs
  `docker compose down -v` for this project (deleting its volumes), only because you asked. By hand:

      docker compose up -d --wait db
      docker compose exec -T db sh -c 'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB"' < DIR/db.sql
      docker compose up -d --wait app
      docker compose exec -T app tar -C /app/media -xzf - < DIR/media.tar.gz

  Another compose project name: `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=name bin/docker-backup.sh`.
- **Reset:** `bin/docker-down.sh --volumes`, then `bin/docker-up.sh`. Never use `docker volume prune`.
- **Dev stack:** `bin/dev-up.sh` starts a separate stack (`docker-compose.dev.yml`, project
  `{{PROJECT_PACKAGE}}-dev`, `127.0.0.1:8880`, `DEV_PORT` changes it): DEBUG and debug toolbar on, source
  bind-mounted with runserver autoreload, PostgreSQL, own volumes. It never touches the production stack.
  `bin/dev-down.sh` keeps its volumes unless `--volumes`.
- **Production vs development packages:** `requirements.txt` is production only; `requirements-dev.txt` adds the
  debug toolbar. The Dockerfile has `dev` and `production` stages (production is the default and contains no
  debug toolbar). `docutils` stays in production because `/admin/docs/` is enabled.
- **Image pins and releases:** the Dockerfile and compose images are pinned by digest (`python:3.12-slim@sha256:...`,
  tag kept readable). `bin/pin-images.sh --check` reports stale pins (registry HTTP API, no Docker daemon needed),
  `bin/pin-images.sh` rewrites them (clean git tree required), `--dry-run` only shows. `bin/release.sh vX.Y.Z`
  refreshes the pins, runs the checks and tests, writes `VERSION` and `CHANGELOG.md`, makes the release commit and
  annotated tag, and PRINTS the push commands (it never pushes). The app image is
  `${APP_IMAGE:-{{PROJECT_PACKAGE}}}:${APP_VERSION:-latest}`.
- **Local SQLite is unchanged:** without `DB_ENGINE=postgres` the settings use `db.sqlite3` and a local cache, so
  the Quick start, `runserver` and `bin/verify.sh` work as before. Opt out of all Docker files at scaffold time
  with `bin/new-site.py --no-docker`.

